```
	ret = sysfs_create_link(&dev->kobj, &partner->dev.kobj, "typec");
	if (ret)
		return;                                  /* silent, no state recorded */
	ret = sysfs_create_link(&partner->dev.kobj, &dev->kobj, dev_name(dev));
	if (ret) {
		sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec");  /* rolled back */
		return;
	}
```
`struct typec_port` (`drivers/usb/typec/class.h`) only stores `struct device *usb2_dev; struct device *usb3_dev;` — there is **no** "link established" flag. So `usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev` stay set even when linking failed/was rolled back, and `typec_unregister_partner()` later unlinks unconditionally → ENOENT.

(b) *No reference on the cached USB device, and a teardown race.* `typec_partner_attach()` just caches the pointer:
```
	struct usb_device *udev = to_usb_device(dev);
	if (udev->speed < USB_SPEED_SUPER)
		port->usb2_dev = dev;
	else
		port->usb3_dev = dev;
```
with no `get_device()`. The clearing path is `typec_partner_deattach()` (`port->usb2_dev == dev` → NULL), reached from `typec_deattach()` (`include/linux/usb/typec.h`) which is called in `usb_disconnect()`:
```
	usb_disable_device(udev, 0);
	typec_deattach(port_dev->connector, &udev->dev);
	usb_remove_ep_devs(&udev->ep0);
	device_del(&udev->dev);
```
`typec_deattach()` is a no-op unless `port_dev->connector` is set (`drivers/usb/core/port.c`, set only in `connector_bind()`, cleared in `connector_unbind()`). So if the component link is unbound, or the USB device dies without going through `port_dev->connector`, `device_del()` frees `udev->dev.kobj.sd` while `port->usb2_dev` still points at it. The subsequent UCSI disconnect event → `typec_unregister_partner()` → `sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec")` on a `sd == NULL` kobject → the exact WARN. `port->partner_link_lock` serialises typec-side callers but gives no protection against USB-core teardown.

At boot on this machine both conditions are easy to hit: the UCSI connector-change work is queued very early (`ucsi_partner_task(con, ucsi_check_connection, 1, 0)` at registration) and races with USB enumeration/disconnect of the Type-C-attached device.

## Is a fix present in this tree?

What *is* present (so those earlier fixes are already in 6.17):
- `mutex partner_link_lock` in `struct typec_port`, taken in `typec_register_partner()`, `typec_unregister_partner()`, `typec_partner_attach()`, `typec_partner_deattach()` — the earlier partner-registration race fix.
- `if (con->partner) return 0;` in `ucsi_register_partner()` and `if (!con->partner) return;` in `ucsi_unregister_partner()` — double-register/unregister guards.

What is **not** present (the actual missing fix for this warning):
- no per-device "link created" state to make `typec_partner_unlink_device()` idempotent,
- no `get_device()`/`put_device()` on `port->usb2_dev` / `port->usb3_dev`,
- no `device_is_registered()` / `dev->kobj.sd` check before `sysfs_remove_link()` (grep for `device_is_registered|kernfs|kobj.sd` in `class.c` returns nothing).

So: **the root cause is unfixed in this tree**, in `typec_partner_unlink_device()` / `typec_unregister_partner()` / `typec_partner_attach()` in `drivers/usb/typec/class.c`.

